1
00:02:32,519 --> 00:02:34,330
To you, Allison.

2
00:02:34,354 --> 00:02:35,898
Thank you.

3
00:02:38,158 --> 00:02:39,236
I picked a great weekend

4
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:40,637
for a coming out
party, didn't I?

5
00:02:40,661 --> 00:02:42,071
Mm. I've lived here

6
00:02:42,095 --> 00:02:44,273
my whole life. I've never
seen a storm like this.

7
00:02:44,297 --> 00:02:47,243
We've never had
anything like this.

8
00:02:47,267 --> 00:02:48,778
Supposed to be the worst series

9
00:02:48,802 --> 00:02:51,080
of storms in the last 100 years.

10
00:02:51,104 --> 00:02:54,084
Oh, boy. Oh, boy.

11
00:02:54,108 --> 00:02:55,807
Oh.

12
00:02:57,544 --> 00:02:58,911
It's cold.

13
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:01,057
You all right, Allison?

14
00:03:01,081 --> 00:03:02,759
Uh, I'm fine, thanks.

15
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:03,760
Can I get you anything?

16
00:03:03,784 --> 00:03:06,363
No. Go and sit down.

17
00:03:06,387 --> 00:03:08,965
Listen, I know you're
all too polite to mention it,

18
00:03:08,989 --> 00:03:11,368
but I'm not ashamed of the fact

19
00:03:11,392 --> 00:03:13,536
that I've been in a sanitarium.

20
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:15,604
And at least not
embarrassed in front of you.

21
00:03:15,628 --> 00:03:17,973
And this time I'm home for good.

22
00:03:17,997 --> 00:03:20,176
And you're doing just fine.

23
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:21,878
Thanks to you, Dr. Linder.

24
00:03:21,902 --> 00:03:24,681
We all care about you, Allison.

25
00:03:24,705 --> 00:03:26,383
And I'm grateful
for all you've done.

26
00:03:26,407 --> 00:03:28,251
You've taken such
good care of everything.

27
00:03:28,275 --> 00:03:30,954
And I don't mean just the
way you've run the house.

28
00:03:30,978 --> 00:03:32,488
You've been like family to me.

29
00:03:32,512 --> 00:03:34,090
And Uncle Bob,

30
00:03:34,114 --> 00:03:36,395
I don't know what I would
have done without you.

31
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:38,461
I'll always be there for you.

32
00:03:38,485 --> 00:03:40,752
You know that.

33
00:03:42,756 --> 00:03:44,366
Hello.

34
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:46,157
Well. It's about time.

35
00:03:48,261 --> 00:03:51,174
Well... made it, finally.

36
00:03:51,198 --> 00:03:52,641
Oh. Sorry I'm late.

37
00:03:52,665 --> 00:03:54,543
Damn roads were flooded.

38
00:03:54,567 --> 00:03:57,646
We were getting
concerned about you.

39
00:03:57,670 --> 00:03:59,482
Yes. I'm sure.

40
00:03:59,506 --> 00:04:01,784
It's lucky I got here.

41
00:04:01,808 --> 00:04:04,254
The state police said they
were gonna close the highway

42
00:04:04,278 --> 00:04:06,256
after they let me through.

43
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:08,592
Oh, Lordy. You mean we're
gonna have to spend the night here?

44
00:04:08,616 --> 00:04:10,794
Oh, I'm sure we can make
everyone very comfortable.

45
00:04:10,818 --> 00:04:12,950
Well, that's good.

46
00:04:14,788 --> 00:04:17,767
I had thought that we would go
over the estate matters tonight,

47
00:04:17,791 --> 00:04:19,268
but in view of the
circumstances,

48
00:04:19,292 --> 00:04:21,704
I think it can wait
until morning.

49
00:04:21,728 --> 00:04:23,540
That is, if all of you can wait.

50
00:04:23,564 --> 00:04:25,763
Uh, I don't understand.

51
00:04:27,834 --> 00:04:31,948
Allison, in anticipation
of your 21st birthday,

52
00:04:31,972 --> 00:04:35,440
I have been doing an extensive
evaluation of the estate.

53
00:04:37,511 --> 00:04:39,455
I'm going to step
down as trustee.

54
00:04:39,479 --> 00:04:41,123
You're going to be in charge.

55
00:04:41,147 --> 00:04:42,992
I think you should have
a full understanding

56
00:04:43,016 --> 00:04:45,550
of just how things
work around here.

57
00:04:50,724 --> 00:04:52,535
I'm ready for my dinner now.

58
00:04:52,559 --> 00:04:54,959
Mm.

59
00:04:57,531 --> 00:05:00,051
Now, don't let me
interrupt your fun.

60
00:06:16,343 --> 00:06:18,663
Allison?

61
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:35,860
Allison.

62
00:07:04,357 --> 00:07:06,677
Allison.

63
00:07:26,546 --> 00:07:28,280
Is anybody there?

64
00:08:52,499 --> 00:08:54,410
Mr. Matlock.

65
00:08:54,434 --> 00:08:55,434
Yeah?

66
00:08:57,904 --> 00:09:00,849
Boy. This is some place.

67
00:09:00,873 --> 00:09:02,685
I've heard of this place.

68
00:09:02,709 --> 00:09:04,620
Of course, everybody has.

69
00:09:04,644 --> 00:09:07,390
Well... Yes, sir.

70
00:09:07,414 --> 00:09:09,057
Ah. Now, sir.

71
00:09:09,081 --> 00:09:11,260
Now, about Allison. Yes?

72
00:09:11,284 --> 00:09:14,130
Oh. Would you like one of these?

73
00:09:14,154 --> 00:09:15,231
I don't smoke.

74
00:09:15,255 --> 00:09:16,887
Understood.

75
00:09:18,891 --> 00:09:20,803
Now, about your niece?

76
00:09:20,827 --> 00:09:23,573
Allison isn't really my niece.

77
00:09:23,597 --> 00:09:27,343
But I have been her "Uncle
Bob" ever since she was a baby.

78
00:09:27,367 --> 00:09:29,778
See, her father and I
were in business together,

79
00:09:29,802 --> 00:09:31,780
until he and his
wife were killed.

80
00:09:31,804 --> 00:09:34,350
You may remember. They
surprised a burglar in the act.

81
00:09:34,374 --> 00:09:37,085
Oh, they got the
guy, didn't they?

82
00:09:37,109 --> 00:09:39,989
Yes, they did,
but unfortunately,

83
00:09:40,013 --> 00:09:42,513
Allison was the one who
discovered the bodies.

84
00:09:43,684 --> 00:09:45,562
And I'm afraid
she's had to spend

85
00:09:45,586 --> 00:09:48,598
a great deal of
time here ever since.

86
00:09:48,622 --> 00:09:51,734
Was coming back
to the sanitarium

87
00:09:51,758 --> 00:09:53,069
a condition of her bail?

88
00:09:53,093 --> 00:09:56,239
No. No, no. The judge
released her into my custody.

89
00:09:56,263 --> 00:09:59,575
But Allison and I both felt
that she'd be better off here.

90
00:10:01,634 --> 00:10:03,312
She's very confused.

91
00:10:03,336 --> 00:10:04,413
Mr. Matlock...

92
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:07,583
she had no reason
to kill Marsha Gold.

93
00:10:07,607 --> 00:10:10,087
She obviously didn't
know what she was doing.

94
00:10:11,278 --> 00:10:13,456
Not guilty by
reason of insanity?

95
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:17,860
And I want you to understand
that money is not an issue here.

96
00:10:17,884 --> 00:10:20,162
As you know,
Allison is an extremely

97
00:10:20,186 --> 00:10:21,485
wealthy young woman.

98
00:10:36,703 --> 00:10:39,148
Um... Allison?

99
00:10:39,172 --> 00:10:42,718
Uh... I'm, uh, Ben Matlock.

100
00:10:42,742 --> 00:10:44,019
I know.

101
00:10:44,043 --> 00:10:45,843
My Uncle Bob told me about you.

102
00:10:56,456 --> 00:11:00,524
Um... may I sit down?

103
00:11:09,702 --> 00:11:13,316
Well, um... Mm.

104
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:18,287
Um... why don't we start...

105
00:11:18,311 --> 00:11:20,256
with your telling me

106
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:23,426
everything you
remember about, um...

107
00:11:23,450 --> 00:11:25,228
that night.

108
00:11:25,252 --> 00:11:27,329
I understand you were
having a dinner party.

109
00:11:27,353 --> 00:11:30,399
It was sort of a coming out.

110
00:11:30,423 --> 00:11:31,733
Dr. Linder arranged it.

111
00:11:31,757 --> 00:11:32,735
Oh, yeah?

112
00:11:32,759 --> 00:11:36,260
Anything... unusual
happen at dinner?

113
00:11:37,697 --> 00:11:41,343
Well, the lights kept going on
and off because of the storm,

114
00:11:41,367 --> 00:11:44,046
and... then the
power finally went out.

115
00:11:44,070 --> 00:11:46,081
And the roads were flooded,

116
00:11:46,105 --> 00:11:47,504
so everyone spent the night.

117
00:11:50,477 --> 00:11:51,776
And then I had the dream.

118
00:11:53,746 --> 00:11:54,724
The dream?

119
00:11:54,748 --> 00:11:57,493
It's always the same.

120
00:11:57,517 --> 00:12:00,985
I go into the library,
and I... I find my mother.

121
00:12:02,455 --> 00:12:03,654
Dead.

122
00:12:06,126 --> 00:12:07,658
Oh, I see.

123
00:12:09,462 --> 00:12:11,974
So the other night
I woke up, and it...

124
00:12:11,998 --> 00:12:13,475
Tsk.

125
00:12:13,499 --> 00:12:15,699
It was like it was
happening all over again.

126
00:12:17,937 --> 00:12:20,257
And then I heard it again.

127
00:12:20,940 --> 00:12:21,940
Um...

128
00:12:26,812 --> 00:12:27,789
Heard what?

129
00:12:27,813 --> 00:12:30,292
My name.

130
00:12:30,316 --> 00:12:32,476
Somebody was calling my name.

131
00:12:33,353 --> 00:12:34,330
Who?

132
00:12:34,354 --> 00:12:35,731
I don't know.

133
00:12:35,755 --> 00:12:37,422
What'd you do?

134
00:12:39,259 --> 00:12:40,969
I-I followed it...

135
00:12:40,993 --> 00:12:44,106
into the library... the voice

136
00:12:44,130 --> 00:12:46,808
and that's...
That's when I saw...

137
00:12:46,832 --> 00:12:48,466
What did you see?

138
00:12:50,904 --> 00:12:52,302
My mother.

139
00:12:54,841 --> 00:12:56,151
I-I know it's impossible.

140
00:12:56,175 --> 00:12:58,187
I know she's dead.
But that's what I saw.

141
00:12:58,211 --> 00:12:59,522
Well...

142
00:12:59,546 --> 00:13:02,624
if the power was off,
it must have been...

143
00:13:02,648 --> 00:13:03,992
very dark.

144
00:13:04,016 --> 00:13:06,628
What made you so sure

145
00:13:06,652 --> 00:13:08,772
it was your mother you saw?

146
00:13:10,090 --> 00:13:12,801
Because I saw the cigarette
holder that she always used,

147
00:13:12,825 --> 00:13:15,226
and the emerald necklace
that she loved to wear.

148
00:13:16,696 --> 00:13:19,564
What happened to those
things after your mother was...?

149
00:13:21,568 --> 00:13:22,733
After she died?

150
00:13:23,803 --> 00:13:25,314
Well, I... I keep the
necklace in my safe,

151
00:13:25,338 --> 00:13:27,883
and... the cigarette holder

152
00:13:27,907 --> 00:13:31,442
in my room in a jewelry
box in my dresser.

153
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:35,057
I-I just do.

154
00:13:35,081 --> 00:13:37,641
Oh, I-I... I can
understand that.

155
00:13:39,986 --> 00:13:42,066
What about the letter opener?

156
00:13:42,788 --> 00:13:44,866
It... It's always on
the desk in the library.

157
00:13:44,890 --> 00:13:46,668
You remember picking it up?

158
00:13:46,692 --> 00:13:48,104
No.

159
00:13:48,128 --> 00:13:49,572
You certain?

160
00:13:49,596 --> 00:13:51,107
Yes.

161
00:13:51,131 --> 00:13:54,832
Allison... do you remember...

162
00:13:57,637 --> 00:14:00,138
stabbing Marsha Gold?

163
00:14:09,015 --> 00:14:10,926
I... I don't know.

164
00:14:10,950 --> 00:14:12,550
I-I just don't know.

165
00:14:28,667 --> 00:14:31,280
Uh, there's no one at
your reception desk here.

166
00:14:31,304 --> 00:14:33,382
Down at the courthouse.

167
00:14:33,406 --> 00:14:34,483
Hi, how are you.

168
00:14:34,507 --> 00:14:36,018
I'm, uh, Warren
Kreitzer from New York.

169
00:14:36,042 --> 00:14:37,486
We... We spoke
on the phone, right?

170
00:14:37,510 --> 00:14:39,454
Oh, yeah. Yeah.

171
00:14:39,478 --> 00:14:40,990
You talked so fast I couldn't

172
00:14:41,014 --> 00:14:42,558
quite make out what
you were saying.

173
00:14:42,582 --> 00:14:44,926
Ha-ha. Well, our firm,
Stevens, Douglas and Jay,

174
00:14:44,950 --> 00:14:46,228
handles Allison Tate's trust.

175
00:14:46,252 --> 00:14:48,097
And with what's
happened, Mr. Douglas felt

176
00:14:48,121 --> 00:14:50,032
that we should have
someone on the scene.

177
00:14:50,056 --> 00:14:51,267
Ah.

178
00:14:51,291 --> 00:14:52,601
Now, I realize
it's not necessary

179
00:14:52,625 --> 00:14:53,869
with you handling things,

180
00:14:53,893 --> 00:14:57,172
but Mr. Douglas is a
stickler for detail, you know?

181
00:14:57,196 --> 00:14:58,341
Oh. Ha-ha.

182
00:14:58,365 --> 00:15:00,397
I was wondering, um, how
you gonna plead Allison?

183
00:15:03,870 --> 00:15:05,114
I don't know.

184
00:15:05,138 --> 00:15:07,349
I was afraid you might
consider pleading her not guilty

185
00:15:07,373 --> 00:15:08,350
by reason of insanity.

186
00:15:08,374 --> 00:15:11,487
Oh. That's a bad idea?

187
00:15:11,511 --> 00:15:13,622
Yeah. Yes. Yes, it is.

188
00:15:13,646 --> 00:15:15,324
Why?

189
00:15:15,348 --> 00:15:17,626
Well, I've been down here
on and off on estate business,

190
00:15:17,650 --> 00:15:19,094
and I've gotten
to know her a little.

191
00:15:19,118 --> 00:15:20,296
I think she's fragile, yeah,

192
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:23,554
but I don't think
she's... unbalanced.

193
00:15:26,659 --> 00:15:27,703
You a psychiatrist?

194
00:15:27,727 --> 00:15:30,438
N... No.

195
00:15:31,463 --> 00:15:32,841
But I notice things, you know?

196
00:15:32,865 --> 00:15:34,777
For example, if I came
into the office of a leading

197
00:15:34,801 --> 00:15:37,112
defense attorney and
found him shining his shoes,

198
00:15:37,136 --> 00:15:39,003
I wouldn't necessarily
think he was compulsive.

199
00:15:40,039 --> 00:15:41,350
What would you think?

200
00:15:41,374 --> 00:15:44,687
Uh, maybe it's tough to get
a good shoeshine in Atlanta.

201
00:15:44,711 --> 00:15:45,711
I don't know.

202
00:15:47,880 --> 00:15:50,459
Well, you'd be wrong.

203
00:15:50,483 --> 00:15:52,828
It's not tough.

204
00:15:52,852 --> 00:15:55,097
And I am compulsive.

205
00:15:55,121 --> 00:15:56,331
I like to shine my shoes.

206
00:15:56,355 --> 00:15:58,267
I... I shine them all the time.

207
00:15:58,291 --> 00:15:59,701
Oh. Well, okay.

208
00:15:59,725 --> 00:16:02,338
Well, heh-heh. I guess I
was wrong about your shoes.

209
00:16:02,362 --> 00:16:04,139
They look fantastic, by the way.

210
00:16:04,163 --> 00:16:05,441
Oh. Thank you. Very nice.

211
00:16:07,467 --> 00:16:10,179
Well, Mr. Kreatzer...
That's "Kreitzer."

212
00:16:10,203 --> 00:16:11,814
Yeah, Kreitzer.

213
00:16:11,838 --> 00:16:13,983
If I ever need expert testimony,

214
00:16:14,007 --> 00:16:15,384
I'll keep you in mind.

215
00:16:15,408 --> 00:16:17,908
Heh. You wouldn't be going
out to the Tate place, would you?

216
00:16:20,947 --> 00:16:21,924
Yeah.

217
00:16:21,948 --> 00:16:23,158
You mind if I come along?

218
00:16:23,182 --> 00:16:24,993
I'd like to, you know,
look the place over.

219
00:16:25,017 --> 00:16:26,595
You got a car?

220
00:16:26,619 --> 00:16:28,753
Heh-heh. Do I ever.

221
00:16:29,922 --> 00:16:30,899
Rental.

222
00:16:30,923 --> 00:16:32,901
You can drive.

223
00:16:51,944 --> 00:16:54,244
It was Mrs. Tate's
favorite piece.

224
00:16:55,314 --> 00:16:58,160
Well, I can see why.

225
00:16:58,184 --> 00:16:59,464
It's a real beauty.

226
00:17:01,187 --> 00:17:02,286
May I, uh...

227
00:17:30,917 --> 00:17:31,982
Thanks.

228
00:17:52,538 --> 00:17:54,083
Huh? Huh?

229
00:17:54,107 --> 00:17:55,951
Yeah. You know, uh, according to

230
00:17:55,975 --> 00:17:57,619
the police report,
there were three people

231
00:17:57,643 --> 00:17:59,221
in the house on the
night of the murder,

232
00:17:59,245 --> 00:18:01,289
a-apart from Allison
and Marsha Gold.

233
00:18:01,313 --> 00:18:03,492
W-when did you
see the police report?

234
00:18:03,516 --> 00:18:05,361
I stopped by the station
on my way to your office.

235
00:18:05,385 --> 00:18:07,095
Oh. Did...?

236
00:18:07,119 --> 00:18:08,764
Do you want to
hear the rest of it?

237
00:18:08,788 --> 00:18:10,365
Okay.

238
00:18:10,389 --> 00:18:12,600
That leaves us three suspects.

239
00:18:12,624 --> 00:18:16,038
Uncle Bob, Doctor
Linder and Mrs. Johnson.

240
00:18:16,062 --> 00:18:17,939
You want to hear a motive?

241
00:18:17,963 --> 00:18:19,441
Okay.

242
00:18:19,465 --> 00:18:22,044
I think Mrs. Johnson's
been stealing from the estate.

243
00:18:22,068 --> 00:18:25,013
That emerald
necklace. It was... Fake.

244
00:18:25,037 --> 00:18:26,415
Oh, you... You
spotted that too, huh?

245
00:18:26,439 --> 00:18:27,439
Yeah.

246
00:18:28,808 --> 00:18:30,907
My uncle's a jeweler. Jeweler?

247
00:18:33,779 --> 00:18:34,979
Um...

248
00:18:36,282 --> 00:18:37,259
What?

249
00:18:37,283 --> 00:18:39,116
Tell me about Uncle Bob.

250
00:18:40,419 --> 00:18:42,797
Marsha Gold was doing
an audit of the estate.

251
00:18:42,821 --> 00:18:44,733
Bob Ranier is the
investment counselor, right?

252
00:18:44,757 --> 00:18:47,502
Maybe he's, you
know, getting creative.

253
00:18:47,526 --> 00:18:48,806
Oh. Yeah.

254
00:18:51,698 --> 00:18:52,778
Dr. Linder?

255
00:18:55,602 --> 00:18:57,122
I don't think we
can rule him out.

256
00:18:58,905 --> 00:19:00,115
Where to?

257
00:19:00,139 --> 00:19:03,385
Sanitarium? Okay.

258
00:19:17,257 --> 00:19:20,269
Uh, this is some place
you've got here, Dr. Linder.

259
00:19:20,293 --> 00:19:22,371
Thank you. We're
very proud of it.

260
00:19:22,395 --> 00:19:24,740
I understand that this is
bigger than your first hospital.

261
00:19:24,764 --> 00:19:27,109
Well, we outgrew
our previous facility.

262
00:19:27,133 --> 00:19:28,577
Oh, I'd say.

263
00:19:28,601 --> 00:19:30,445
What is this, 15, 20 acres?

264
00:19:30,469 --> 00:19:32,314
Mm, about. Oh-ho.

265
00:19:32,338 --> 00:19:34,316
Some of the most exclusive

266
00:19:34,340 --> 00:19:36,485
residential property in Atlanta,

267
00:19:36,509 --> 00:19:38,620
no wonder you have
to charge your patients.

268
00:19:38,644 --> 00:19:40,622
We haven't had any complaints.

269
00:19:40,646 --> 00:19:44,293
Our clientele is
very... comfortable.

270
00:19:44,317 --> 00:19:45,627
Yeah, and I bet Allison Tate

271
00:19:45,651 --> 00:19:49,431
is one of the most
comfortable patients you've got.

272
00:19:49,455 --> 00:19:51,600
I really wouldn't know.

273
00:19:51,624 --> 00:19:53,769
You know, the talk around town

274
00:19:53,793 --> 00:19:56,738
is that, uh, right
after she became

275
00:19:56,762 --> 00:19:59,041
your patient, you
were able to...

276
00:19:59,065 --> 00:20:00,309
break ground on it.

277
00:20:00,333 --> 00:20:02,044
I fail to see what one
has to do with the other.

278
00:20:02,068 --> 00:20:04,579
I-I-I-I'm just... I'm just
trying to put this together.

279
00:20:04,603 --> 00:20:06,181
I'm not saying
there's a connection.

280
00:20:06,205 --> 00:20:08,150
But it does seem interesting

281
00:20:08,174 --> 00:20:10,685
that right after one
of the richest people

282
00:20:10,709 --> 00:20:12,454
in the state
became your patient,

283
00:20:12,478 --> 00:20:14,990
you were able to
build this sanitarium.

284
00:20:15,014 --> 00:20:16,525
Well, this place has
been a dream of mine

285
00:20:16,549 --> 00:20:17,526
for years, Mr. Matlock.

286
00:20:17,550 --> 00:20:20,551
Well... that's good.

287
00:20:22,054 --> 00:20:24,165
Uh, that's, uh, Allison's
bungalow over there?

288
00:20:24,189 --> 00:20:26,601
Uh, right over
there, sir. Mm-hm.

289
00:20:26,625 --> 00:20:28,970
I'll tell you one thing.

290
00:20:28,994 --> 00:20:31,540
If I ever lose it,
this is the place.

291
00:20:31,564 --> 00:20:33,342
If I can get a deal.

292
00:20:36,669 --> 00:20:39,214
Warren, I'm just so scared.

293
00:20:39,238 --> 00:20:42,017
Oh. Allison, Ben
Matlock is very good.

294
00:20:42,041 --> 00:20:45,487
Really. You couldn't
be in better hands.

295
00:20:45,511 --> 00:20:46,855
Unless, of course,
they were mine.

296
00:20:49,015 --> 00:20:51,260
Thanks.

297
00:20:51,284 --> 00:20:52,428
It's real nice you came down.

298
00:20:52,452 --> 00:20:53,452
I really appreciate it.

299
00:20:57,423 --> 00:20:59,134
Oh. Oh, that's right.

300
00:20:59,158 --> 00:21:00,135
You know each other.

301
00:21:00,159 --> 00:21:01,236
I was just telling Allison

302
00:21:01,260 --> 00:21:03,005
how lucky she is to have
you representing her.

303
00:21:03,029 --> 00:21:04,261
Oh?

304
00:21:06,098 --> 00:21:07,475
Hang in there, kid. Okay?

305
00:21:07,499 --> 00:21:08,543
Okay. I'll see you later.

306
00:21:08,567 --> 00:21:09,900
Bye.

307
00:21:17,843 --> 00:21:19,075
Allison.

308
00:21:22,248 --> 00:21:23,892
Are you sure...

309
00:21:23,916 --> 00:21:27,429
the cigarette holder
was in the jewelry box?

310
00:21:27,453 --> 00:21:29,998
It... It was there
that afternoon.

311
00:21:30,022 --> 00:21:31,834
It wasn't there this morning.

312
00:21:33,559 --> 00:21:34,859
I... I don't understand.

313
00:21:36,595 --> 00:21:39,107
I think Marsha Gold
was already dead...

314
00:21:39,131 --> 00:21:42,044
before you got to the library.

315
00:21:42,068 --> 00:21:45,647
Somebody used the necklace

316
00:21:45,671 --> 00:21:47,516
and cigarette holder as props.

317
00:21:47,540 --> 00:21:50,385
Was in a hurry to
get the necklace

318
00:21:50,409 --> 00:21:52,543
back into the safe,
put this in there with it.

319
00:21:54,647 --> 00:21:56,291
Somebody else killed her?

320
00:21:56,315 --> 00:21:58,193
Yeah.

321
00:21:58,217 --> 00:22:00,161
Somebody else killed her.

322
00:22:00,185 --> 00:22:01,452
Mr. Matlock.

323
00:22:03,055 --> 00:22:04,466
Allison needs her rest.

324
00:22:04,490 --> 00:22:07,102
Oh.

325
00:22:07,126 --> 00:22:08,804
I'll go now.

326
00:22:08,828 --> 00:22:09,871
Thank you.

327
00:22:09,895 --> 00:22:12,941
Yep. Talk to you later.

328
00:22:23,776 --> 00:22:27,622
I'm sorry, but we pride
ourselves on our discretion.

329
00:22:27,646 --> 00:22:28,990
I'm sure you understand.

330
00:22:29,014 --> 00:22:30,525
Of course. Heh-heh. Of course.

331
00:22:30,549 --> 00:22:32,694
And I'd never ask you to
compromise your position,

332
00:22:32,718 --> 00:22:34,696
Mrs... "Walters."

333
00:22:34,720 --> 00:22:35,697
Oh. Nancy.

334
00:22:35,721 --> 00:22:38,166
And it's "miss."

335
00:22:38,190 --> 00:22:39,968
Nancy.

336
00:22:39,992 --> 00:22:42,637
That's very nice. But
you see, Nancy, uh,

337
00:22:42,661 --> 00:22:45,407
I'm working with Ben Matlock.
We're defending Allison Tate.

338
00:22:45,431 --> 00:22:48,610
Allison? Oh, that poor girl.

339
00:22:48,634 --> 00:22:51,412
So if there's anything
at all you could tell me...

340
00:22:51,436 --> 00:22:53,115
May I help you? Not at all.

341
00:22:53,139 --> 00:22:54,515
Thanks. I'll try
that restaurant.

342
00:22:54,539 --> 00:22:56,073
Thank you. Nancy.

343
00:23:00,112 --> 00:23:01,790
Have a nice day.

344
00:23:11,357 --> 00:23:14,002
Hm. What you been doing?

345
00:23:14,026 --> 00:23:15,671
Well, it occurred to
me it takes major bucks

346
00:23:15,695 --> 00:23:17,172
to fund a setup like this.

347
00:23:17,196 --> 00:23:18,640
And having someone
like Allison Tate

348
00:23:18,664 --> 00:23:20,275
in and out of this
place on a regular basis,

349
00:23:20,299 --> 00:23:22,344
it's almost like
having an annuity. Oh.

350
00:23:22,368 --> 00:23:24,213
I'd like to figure a way
to get a look at his books,

351
00:23:24,237 --> 00:23:25,517
you know what I mean?

352
00:23:28,241 --> 00:23:29,539
Hey, Warren?

353
00:23:31,043 --> 00:23:33,254
I appreciate that you
think Allison's innocent.

354
00:23:33,278 --> 00:23:35,390
I do too.

355
00:23:35,414 --> 00:23:36,391
You do?

356
00:23:36,415 --> 00:23:37,559
Yeah.

357
00:23:37,583 --> 00:23:39,728
Well... that's good news.

358
00:23:39,752 --> 00:23:41,062
Right?

359
00:23:44,023 --> 00:23:45,103
Now we're cooking with gas.

360
00:23:59,438 --> 00:24:00,438
Hi, can I help you?

361
00:24:03,008 --> 00:24:04,008
Who are you?

362
00:24:06,245 --> 00:24:07,555
You must be Tyler Hudson.

363
00:24:08,914 --> 00:24:11,026
You don't know how much I
looked forward to meeting you.

364
00:24:11,050 --> 00:24:13,095
And why is that,
Mr... Warren Kreitzer.

365
00:24:13,119 --> 00:24:15,197
I'm down from New York
helping Ben out on the Tate case.

366
00:24:15,221 --> 00:24:16,230
Oh, really?

367
00:24:16,254 --> 00:24:17,732
Yeah, our firm
handles the Tate trust.

368
00:24:17,756 --> 00:24:19,234
You know what?
The way I hear it,

369
00:24:19,258 --> 00:24:21,470
you're a big part of the reason
for Ben Matlock's success.

370
00:24:21,494 --> 00:24:24,339
Ah, I... I wouldn't
say all that.

371
00:24:24,363 --> 00:24:26,074
Come on, let's face it, Tyler...

372
00:24:26,098 --> 00:24:27,642
I can call you
Tyler, can't I? Yeah.

373
00:24:27,666 --> 00:24:29,410
Well, you and I both
know that in criminal law,

374
00:24:29,434 --> 00:24:30,879
a good investigator
can be a lawyer's

375
00:24:30,903 --> 00:24:32,347
most valuable asset.

376
00:24:32,371 --> 00:24:34,716
Well, I like to feel
like I do my bit.

377
00:24:34,740 --> 00:24:36,084
"My bit." What,
are you kidding me?

378
00:24:36,108 --> 00:24:37,552
What about the...
The Beckerman case?

379
00:24:37,576 --> 00:24:39,821
Yeah, the Beckerman case.

380
00:24:39,845 --> 00:24:42,090
They never could have gotten
an acquittal without the work

381
00:24:42,114 --> 00:24:43,692
you did on that.
That was a tough one.

382
00:24:43,716 --> 00:24:44,693
Yeah.

383
00:24:44,717 --> 00:24:46,394
Speaking of tough ones,

384
00:24:46,418 --> 00:24:48,096
this, uh... This Tate
case is a real problem.

385
00:24:48,120 --> 00:24:49,998
Can I buy you a cup of coffee?

386
00:24:50,022 --> 00:24:52,067
I'd like to talk to you
about a couple things.

387
00:25:15,948 --> 00:25:18,226
Hey. What exactly
are we looking for?

388
00:25:18,250 --> 00:25:19,261
Don't worry about it, man.

389
00:25:19,285 --> 00:25:20,862
All you gotta... Gotta
do is get me inside.

390
00:25:20,886 --> 00:25:22,630
I can take it from there.

391
00:25:22,654 --> 00:25:24,966
I don't know where
you went to school.

392
00:25:24,990 --> 00:25:26,901
Okay, here. Hold this.

393
00:25:26,925 --> 00:25:29,793
I'm sure breaking and entering
wasn't part of the curriculum.

394
00:25:31,229 --> 00:25:33,429
All right, here we go.

395
00:25:34,967 --> 00:25:36,277
Yeah.

396
00:25:42,841 --> 00:25:44,485
Bingo.

397
00:25:44,509 --> 00:25:46,220
Whoa.

398
00:25:46,244 --> 00:25:47,822
Great work, Tyler. Thanks.

399
00:25:47,846 --> 00:25:50,366
Are you absolutely
sure about this?

400
00:25:50,783 --> 00:25:52,994
Go on, go on. Beat it. Be...
Before we both get caught, okay?

401
00:25:53,018 --> 00:25:55,418
All right. You're on your own.

402
00:26:56,181 --> 00:26:57,926
Hi. Heh.

403
00:27:20,940 --> 00:27:22,138
Hi.

404
00:27:24,977 --> 00:27:27,055
About last night, I
just... Now, listen!

405
00:27:27,079 --> 00:27:30,425
When you broke
into that sanitarium,

406
00:27:30,449 --> 00:27:32,927
you were representing
this office and me.

407
00:27:32,951 --> 00:27:34,762
You can be disbarred.

408
00:27:34,786 --> 00:27:36,697
Now, I don't give a
merry damn about that.

409
00:27:36,721 --> 00:27:40,268
But you might have fatally
compromised Allison Tate's case

410
00:27:40,292 --> 00:27:41,503
and what I'm trying to do here.

411
00:27:41,527 --> 00:27:43,604
And I do give a
merry damn about that.

412
00:27:43,628 --> 00:27:45,474
Are they gonna
prosecute...? I don't know.

413
00:27:45,498 --> 00:27:48,676
I talked my head
off to Dr. Linder.

414
00:27:48,700 --> 00:27:50,612
I hope not. For me, not for you.

415
00:27:50,636 --> 00:27:51,847
Well, I appreciate what you...

416
00:27:51,871 --> 00:27:53,515
Now, I've called
Stevens, Douglas and Jay.

417
00:27:53,539 --> 00:27:55,950
They didn't send you
down here to troubleshoot

418
00:27:55,974 --> 00:27:57,351
the Allison Tate murder case.

419
00:27:57,375 --> 00:27:59,320
You're supposed to be
on vacation somewhere.

420
00:27:59,344 --> 00:28:00,655
Why don't you just go there?

421
00:28:00,679 --> 00:28:03,591
Look, I know I screwed
up, and I'm sorry,

422
00:28:03,615 --> 00:28:05,160
but I... I wasn't doing
it just to be cute.

423
00:28:05,184 --> 00:28:07,262
I've known Allison for almost
two years, and although

424
00:28:07,286 --> 00:28:08,863
I've never been in
a position to prove it,

425
00:28:08,887 --> 00:28:10,632
I always thought that
these people around her

426
00:28:10,656 --> 00:28:11,733
were exploiting her.

427
00:28:11,757 --> 00:28:13,468
You and I know that
Mrs. Johnson was doing it.

428
00:28:13,492 --> 00:28:15,002
And I think I can show
you that Dr. Linder

429
00:28:15,026 --> 00:28:17,161
and good old Uncle
Bob had something going.

430
00:28:18,830 --> 00:28:21,509
Here's my report on what
I read in Dr. Linder's office.

431
00:28:21,533 --> 00:28:23,433
Aren't you gonna look at it?

432
00:28:27,406 --> 00:28:29,272
How about now? I mean...

433
00:28:31,043 --> 00:28:32,787
I'll handle it from here.

434
00:28:32,811 --> 00:28:35,190
No appeal, huh?

435
00:28:37,883 --> 00:28:39,594
Okay.

436
00:28:39,618 --> 00:28:43,419
Well, it's been... brief.

437
00:28:46,759 --> 00:28:48,519
I'm sorry I caused
you any problems.

438
00:29:27,766 --> 00:29:29,911
Oh, well. Mr. Matlock.

439
00:29:29,935 --> 00:29:32,013
Would you care for anything?

440
00:29:32,037 --> 00:29:33,369
Oh. No, thank you.

441
00:29:34,606 --> 00:29:35,883
Hm.

442
00:29:35,907 --> 00:29:38,719
This rain. It sure is
something, isn't it?

443
00:29:38,743 --> 00:29:40,321
Yeah.

444
00:29:40,345 --> 00:29:43,458
You said that you had, um...

445
00:29:43,482 --> 00:29:45,360
something important
that you wanted to tell me.

446
00:29:45,384 --> 00:29:47,561
Something that will
help Allison's case.

447
00:29:47,585 --> 00:29:48,797
Well, it might.

448
00:29:48,821 --> 00:29:53,135
It's about Dr. Linder's
sanitarium.

449
00:29:53,159 --> 00:29:54,803
Is something wrong?

450
00:29:54,827 --> 00:29:56,559
I don't know.

451
00:29:58,496 --> 00:30:00,641
Uh, does it strike
you as curious

452
00:30:00,665 --> 00:30:05,680
that the sanitarium happened...

453
00:30:05,704 --> 00:30:08,950
uh, just after Allison
started seeing Dr. Linder?

454
00:30:08,974 --> 00:30:10,840
No, not especially.

455
00:30:12,411 --> 00:30:15,289
Y-you've invested
some of her money

456
00:30:15,313 --> 00:30:16,791
in the sanitarium, haven't you?

457
00:30:16,815 --> 00:30:20,395
Heh. Who have you
been talking to? Didn't you?

458
00:30:20,419 --> 00:30:21,762
It's a good investment.

459
00:30:21,786 --> 00:30:23,798
Well, I guess.

460
00:30:23,822 --> 00:30:25,862
Considering what he charges.

461
00:30:27,726 --> 00:30:29,326
Yeah, wait, wait, wait. Uh...

462
00:30:29,995 --> 00:30:32,373
Do you feel that
there's some sort

463
00:30:32,397 --> 00:30:34,131
of impropriety here?

464
00:30:35,834 --> 00:30:39,213
Well... it's the kind of...

465
00:30:39,237 --> 00:30:41,805
coincidence that bothers me.

466
00:30:45,844 --> 00:30:47,722
Did I tell you I'm gonna
plead her not guilty?

467
00:30:47,746 --> 00:30:49,324
No.

468
00:30:49,348 --> 00:30:51,748
I don't think she
killed anybody.

469
00:31:03,162 --> 00:31:04,162
All packed?

470
00:31:06,865 --> 00:31:07,865
Hey.

471
00:31:10,269 --> 00:31:12,802
Hey. What's wrong?

472
00:31:14,806 --> 00:31:16,117
I don't know if I should go.

473
00:31:17,376 --> 00:31:20,870
Allison... you can't
hide here forever.

474
00:31:22,280 --> 00:31:23,925
Uh, suppose this
is where I belong.

475
00:31:23,949 --> 00:31:25,460
I-I... I don't wanna hear that.

476
00:31:25,484 --> 00:31:27,162
I... I don't even
want you to think it.

477
00:31:27,186 --> 00:31:29,686
Warren, I don't know
what do think anymore.

478
00:31:31,823 --> 00:31:34,835
Sooner or later you're
gonna have to leave here...

479
00:31:34,859 --> 00:31:37,594
because there's nothing
really wrong with you.

480
00:31:39,798 --> 00:31:41,109
Allison, I know you're scared.

481
00:31:41,133 --> 00:31:42,944
We're all scared sometimes.

482
00:31:42,968 --> 00:31:46,247
Even someone as
incredibly brave as myself. I...

483
00:31:46,271 --> 00:31:47,482
I was scared once.

484
00:31:49,408 --> 00:31:50,385
Only once?

485
00:31:50,409 --> 00:31:54,288
Well... maybe twice. I...

486
00:31:54,312 --> 00:31:56,947
Oh, I don't know Warren. I
just want this all to go away.

487
00:31:58,017 --> 00:31:59,427
Well, I'm...

488
00:31:59,451 --> 00:32:01,196
I'm afraid that's not
the way it works, kiddo.

489
00:32:01,220 --> 00:32:05,133
You know? But till
it does... I'll be here.

490
00:32:08,093 --> 00:32:09,593
Thanks.

491
00:32:17,202 --> 00:32:18,402
Shall we dance?

492
00:32:19,538 --> 00:32:20,648
Yeah.

493
00:32:20,672 --> 00:32:21,771
Let's go.

494
00:32:22,874 --> 00:32:24,118
I got it.

495
00:32:45,397 --> 00:32:47,678
Allison.

496
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:49,280
Allison.

497
00:32:51,070 --> 00:32:52,680
No.

498
00:32:52,704 --> 00:32:53,881
I can't.

499
00:32:53,905 --> 00:32:55,083
You have to.

500
00:32:55,107 --> 00:32:57,351
You do.

501
00:32:57,375 --> 00:32:59,654
It's the only way to find out
what happened that night.

502
00:33:03,615 --> 00:33:05,935
Please, what...?
What did you do next?

503
00:33:08,019 --> 00:33:09,419
I started down the stairs.

504
00:33:10,789 --> 00:33:12,066
Okay.

505
00:33:12,090 --> 00:33:13,289
Let's go.

506
00:33:33,011 --> 00:33:35,357
Everything's gonna
be just fine, okay?

507
00:33:35,381 --> 00:33:37,124
We'll just take it
easy. Can you do this?

508
00:33:37,148 --> 00:33:38,860
You all right?

509
00:33:38,884 --> 00:33:41,563
Come on. Let's get
this done. All right?

510
00:33:41,587 --> 00:33:43,130
Warren, do we have to do this?

511
00:33:43,154 --> 00:33:45,099
Yes. Yes. I think so.

512
00:33:45,123 --> 00:33:47,443
I think it's
important, don't you?

513
00:33:50,295 --> 00:33:52,640
Allison. Are you all right?

514
00:33:52,664 --> 00:33:54,075
We were so worried about you.

515
00:33:54,099 --> 00:33:55,643
I'm fine, really.

516
00:33:55,667 --> 00:33:57,178
What the hell do you
think you're doing?

517
00:33:57,202 --> 00:33:59,514
Who gave you the right to take
Allison out of the sanitarium?

518
00:33:59,538 --> 00:34:01,549
She did. Allison.

519
00:34:01,573 --> 00:34:03,618
I don't believe it.
Get out of here.

520
00:34:03,642 --> 00:34:04,619
Just get out of here.

521
00:34:04,643 --> 00:34:06,020
You have no right to be here.

522
00:34:06,044 --> 00:34:07,789
Allison. Allison, listen to me.

523
00:34:07,813 --> 00:34:10,391
All of these people wanna
keep you institutionalized.

524
00:34:10,415 --> 00:34:12,793
Mrs. Johnson, Dr. Linder
and your Uncle Bob.

525
00:34:12,817 --> 00:34:14,362
It's all very clear, Allison.

526
00:34:14,386 --> 00:34:15,696
They're after your money.

527
00:34:15,720 --> 00:34:17,831
Don't you realize the
harm you're doing her.

528
00:34:17,855 --> 00:34:19,200
Please, stop it!

529
00:34:19,224 --> 00:34:21,502
One of you met with Marsha Gold

530
00:34:21,526 --> 00:34:22,870
in the library that night.

531
00:34:22,894 --> 00:34:25,106
She confronted you with what
she knew, and you killed her.

532
00:34:25,130 --> 00:34:26,507
And then you tried
to frame Allison.

533
00:34:26,531 --> 00:34:27,842
Please, stop it.

534
00:34:27,866 --> 00:34:29,176
No.

535
00:34:29,200 --> 00:34:33,581
Allison, you... You can't
possibly believe him.

536
00:34:33,605 --> 00:34:35,182
I do believe him.

537
00:34:35,206 --> 00:34:37,352
Wh...? Allison.

538
00:34:37,376 --> 00:34:39,496
Well...

539
00:34:40,746 --> 00:34:42,423
Good evening.

540
00:34:42,447 --> 00:34:43,646
Good evening.

541
00:34:47,386 --> 00:34:49,466
You don't give up, do you?

542
00:34:50,522 --> 00:34:52,233
Well, folks, uh,

543
00:34:52,257 --> 00:34:54,535
my client, my associate and I

544
00:34:54,559 --> 00:34:56,371
have some things
we need to discuss.

545
00:34:56,395 --> 00:34:57,638
So if you'll excuse us,

546
00:34:57,662 --> 00:35:00,174
we'll see all of you
in court tomorrow.

547
00:35:26,758 --> 00:35:31,673
Okay. Okay, let's...
Let's... Let's go ahead.

548
00:35:31,697 --> 00:35:34,409
You were retracing
your steps, Allison.

549
00:35:34,433 --> 00:35:35,809
Let's... Let's go ahead.

550
00:35:35,833 --> 00:35:37,811
Yeah. Yeah, Allison.

551
00:35:37,835 --> 00:35:38,912
You're here.

552
00:35:38,936 --> 00:35:41,081
You made it this far, right?

553
00:35:41,105 --> 00:35:43,817
Come on. I know you can do it.

554
00:35:43,841 --> 00:35:44,818
How about it?

555
00:35:44,842 --> 00:35:47,187
I'll... I'll try.

556
00:35:47,211 --> 00:35:48,889
Okay.

557
00:35:48,913 --> 00:35:50,758
All right.

558
00:35:52,884 --> 00:35:55,863
Now, just try and visualize

559
00:35:55,887 --> 00:35:58,468
the room exactly the
way it was that night.

560
00:36:02,661 --> 00:36:03,859
All right?

561
00:36:05,030 --> 00:36:06,440
Now, is...?

562
00:36:06,464 --> 00:36:07,942
Is that about where you stopped

563
00:36:07,966 --> 00:36:10,344
when you came in?

564
00:36:11,536 --> 00:36:13,380
A little further, I think.

565
00:36:13,404 --> 00:36:14,971
Okay.

566
00:36:16,808 --> 00:36:19,368
What did you see?

567
00:36:20,378 --> 00:36:21,422
Nothing at first.

568
00:36:21,446 --> 00:36:22,490
It was too dark.

569
00:36:25,284 --> 00:36:26,315
Oh.

570
00:36:35,561 --> 00:36:37,394
It was raining
hard, like it is now.

571
00:36:42,568 --> 00:36:45,034
Then I started to move forward.

572
00:36:49,908 --> 00:36:51,468
And...

573
00:36:57,048 --> 00:36:58,158
I can't.

574
00:36:58,182 --> 00:37:00,350
I can't.

575
00:37:06,358 --> 00:37:08,191
No.

576
00:37:16,668 --> 00:37:18,148
What did you see in the mirror?

577
00:37:21,572 --> 00:37:23,239
You don't remember.

578
00:37:25,710 --> 00:37:27,043
It was too painful.

579
00:37:35,754 --> 00:37:37,419
It was your Uncle
Bob, wasn't it?

580
00:37:46,498 --> 00:37:48,743
Now... Mr. Ranier.

581
00:37:48,767 --> 00:37:51,179
How long have you known...

582
00:37:51,203 --> 00:37:53,614
Allison Tate, the defendant?

583
00:37:53,638 --> 00:37:56,217
Since she was a little girl.

584
00:37:56,241 --> 00:37:58,786
Her father, David, and I
were business partners.

585
00:37:58,810 --> 00:38:00,788
And you have invested

586
00:38:00,812 --> 00:38:05,660
uh, large amounts of her...

587
00:38:05,684 --> 00:38:07,461
considerable fortune
for her, haven't you?

588
00:38:07,485 --> 00:38:08,729
Yes, I have.

589
00:38:08,753 --> 00:38:10,498
I'm the investment
counselor for the estate.

590
00:38:10,522 --> 00:38:11,899
Oh. Yep.

591
00:38:11,923 --> 00:38:13,000
Now... Now, when... When, uh,

592
00:38:13,024 --> 00:38:16,036
Allison turned 21
a few months ago,

593
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:20,141
she was supposed to
take over control of her...

594
00:38:20,165 --> 00:38:21,564
Her own inheritance, wasn't she?

595
00:38:22,634 --> 00:38:24,178
Yes.

596
00:38:24,202 --> 00:38:27,515
And that could mean replacing
you as investment counselor.

597
00:38:27,539 --> 00:38:29,016
Yes.

598
00:38:29,040 --> 00:38:31,185
If she were not
institutionalized

599
00:38:31,209 --> 00:38:33,588
and were of, uh, sound mind,

600
00:38:33,612 --> 00:38:37,358
she could, uh, call for auditors
and accountants, couldn't she?

601
00:38:37,382 --> 00:38:38,826
Objection. Relevance,
Your Honor.

602
00:38:38,850 --> 00:38:41,763
Uh... my client's mental state

603
00:38:41,787 --> 00:38:44,665
is crucial to our
defense, Your Honor.

604
00:38:44,689 --> 00:38:45,699
Overruled.

605
00:38:45,723 --> 00:38:46,700
Answer the question.

606
00:38:46,724 --> 00:38:49,937
The answer is yes. Yeah. Yes.

607
00:38:49,961 --> 00:38:56,044
All right, now,
Allison is... 21...

608
00:38:56,068 --> 00:39:00,014
uh, but she has
been incapacitated.

609
00:39:00,038 --> 00:39:05,686
And... we know that she
was in a mental institution.

610
00:39:05,710 --> 00:39:10,691
So your control was
reactivated, wasn't it?

611
00:39:10,715 --> 00:39:13,260
It's true. That's how her
father set up the estate.

612
00:39:13,284 --> 00:39:15,429
Because he trusted
his business associate

613
00:39:15,453 --> 00:39:17,097
to look after his daughter.

614
00:39:17,121 --> 00:39:18,432
That's right.

615
00:39:18,456 --> 00:39:21,657
And Allison has
needed help, hasn't she?

616
00:39:22,727 --> 00:39:24,538
Unfortunately.

617
00:39:24,562 --> 00:39:26,774
But in every case, I've
seen to it that she's had

618
00:39:26,798 --> 00:39:28,958
the very, very best of care.

619
00:39:30,135 --> 00:39:32,168
At her own expense.

620
00:39:34,005 --> 00:39:36,150
At Linder Sanitarium.

621
00:39:36,174 --> 00:39:39,520
Well... her father's
will stipulates

622
00:39:39,544 --> 00:39:42,022
that the payment for it
comes out of her inheritance.

623
00:39:42,046 --> 00:39:43,624
Yeah. Yeah.

624
00:39:43,648 --> 00:39:46,494
Now, also you have invested...

625
00:39:46,518 --> 00:39:50,364
uh, large amounts
of her inheritance

626
00:39:50,388 --> 00:39:51,699
in Linder Sanitarium.

627
00:39:51,723 --> 00:39:54,335
That right? Indeed, I have.

628
00:39:54,359 --> 00:39:56,971
And the return for that
investment was that Dr. Linder

629
00:39:56,995 --> 00:39:58,305
agreed...

630
00:39:58,329 --> 00:40:02,276
to unnecessarily
institutionalize her?

631
00:40:02,300 --> 00:40:04,945
- How dare you?
- Objection.

632
00:40:04,969 --> 00:40:05,979
Argumentative.

633
00:40:06,003 --> 00:40:07,348
Speculative, Your Honor.

634
00:40:07,372 --> 00:40:08,438
Withdraw the question.

635
00:40:09,407 --> 00:40:10,407
Withdraw it.

636
00:40:12,544 --> 00:40:14,522
Mr. Ranier...

637
00:40:14,546 --> 00:40:18,648
I'll bet if a thorough
accounting...

638
00:40:21,886 --> 00:40:25,866
of Allison's
inheritance were called,

639
00:40:25,890 --> 00:40:28,702
it would show that
you've been dipping into it

640
00:40:28,726 --> 00:40:31,539
for your own profit
for a long time.

641
00:40:31,563 --> 00:40:33,774
And that's why you wanted
to keep her institutionalized.

642
00:40:33,798 --> 00:40:35,075
Your Honor.

643
00:40:35,099 --> 00:40:37,278
Your Honor. May I go
ahead with this, please?

644
00:40:37,302 --> 00:40:38,613
Do you have anywhere to go?

645
00:40:38,637 --> 00:40:39,713
Oh, you betcha. Yes, ma'am.

646
00:40:39,737 --> 00:40:41,081
Overruled.

647
00:40:41,105 --> 00:40:42,404
Proceed.

648
00:40:44,341 --> 00:40:45,586
Thank you.

649
00:40:45,610 --> 00:40:47,254
Marsha Gold found
out about it, didn't she?

650
00:40:47,278 --> 00:40:50,958
She discovered that you
were using Allison's money

651
00:40:50,982 --> 00:40:52,259
for your own gain.

652
00:40:52,283 --> 00:40:53,894
It's nonsense.

653
00:40:53,918 --> 00:40:55,317
I would like...

654
00:40:56,688 --> 00:41:00,100
I'd like to submit as
the Defense Exhibit G,

655
00:41:00,124 --> 00:41:03,504
this study of
Allison Tate's estate,

656
00:41:03,528 --> 00:41:07,463
prepared by the accountant
firm of, uh, Longstreet and Bond.

657
00:41:08,966 --> 00:41:11,006
Allison didn't call
for an accounting.

658
00:41:11,703 --> 00:41:12,913
Somebody else did.

659
00:41:12,937 --> 00:41:15,549
And here in the conclusion,

660
00:41:15,573 --> 00:41:16,784
it states, and I quote:

661
00:41:16,808 --> 00:41:20,121
"There is a pattern of
misappropriation of funds

662
00:41:20,145 --> 00:41:23,090
"by the investment
counselor, Robert Ranier,

663
00:41:23,114 --> 00:41:26,927
"whereby Mr. Ranier
used Allison Tate's money

664
00:41:26,951 --> 00:41:29,997
for his own personal
gain." End quote.

665
00:41:30,021 --> 00:41:32,133
Isn't that why you
killed Marsha Gold

666
00:41:32,157 --> 00:41:33,767
and framed Allison
for the murder? What?

667
00:41:33,791 --> 00:41:35,102
Your Honor, objection.

668
00:41:35,126 --> 00:41:37,037
May I please go ahead with this?

669
00:41:37,061 --> 00:41:38,606
Are you sure you
can get somewhere...?

670
00:41:38,630 --> 00:41:39,807
I'll give you $50 if I'm wrong.

671
00:41:39,831 --> 00:41:40,908
I'll cite you.

672
00:41:40,932 --> 00:41:42,531
I'm sorry. I'm sure.

673
00:41:43,701 --> 00:41:46,135
Very well. Go ahead.

674
00:41:47,205 --> 00:41:49,316
Thank you.

675
00:41:49,340 --> 00:41:52,653
This accounting, which I quoted,

676
00:41:52,677 --> 00:41:55,222
was received by Marsha Gold

677
00:41:55,246 --> 00:41:56,779
the day she died.

678
00:41:58,884 --> 00:42:00,961
You killed her,
and I can prove it.

679
00:42:00,985 --> 00:42:02,763
That's absolutely absurd!

680
00:42:02,787 --> 00:42:04,465
I have a witness.

681
00:42:04,489 --> 00:42:05,633
Allison Tate.

682
00:42:05,657 --> 00:42:08,869
Mr. Matlock, why are
you doing this to me?

683
00:42:08,893 --> 00:42:11,672
For years, Allison has been
imagining all sorts of things.

684
00:42:11,696 --> 00:42:13,307
That's why she
was... Was put away?

685
00:42:13,331 --> 00:42:14,842
No, no, no.

686
00:42:14,866 --> 00:42:17,678
But obviously this is just
another one of her delusions.

687
00:42:17,702 --> 00:42:20,203
Oh, no. No-no-no.

688
00:42:21,272 --> 00:42:22,249
No.

689
00:42:22,273 --> 00:42:23,873
Here's the way it happened.

690
00:42:26,344 --> 00:42:31,826
Allison... was asleep...

691
00:42:31,850 --> 00:42:33,349
in her room that night.

692
00:42:35,619 --> 00:42:38,354
She heard a voice
calling her name.

693
00:42:39,891 --> 00:42:41,557
A whispery voice.

694
00:42:43,494 --> 00:42:45,972
In the middle of the night.

695
00:42:45,996 --> 00:42:47,963
Calling her downstairs.

696
00:42:50,768 --> 00:42:53,848
Now, down in the library...

697
00:42:53,872 --> 00:42:57,017
Marsha Gold was already dead.

698
00:42:57,041 --> 00:42:58,741
Propped up in a chair.

699
00:43:00,078 --> 00:43:03,157
Wearing the necklace
Allison's mother always wore.

700
00:43:03,181 --> 00:43:07,494
Holding the cigarette holder
Allison's mother always held.

701
00:43:07,518 --> 00:43:09,697
And when Allison...

702
00:43:09,721 --> 00:43:12,600
already extremely vulnerable,

703
00:43:12,624 --> 00:43:14,969
walked into that library

704
00:43:14,993 --> 00:43:17,938
and saw what she thought

705
00:43:17,962 --> 00:43:22,710
was her dead mother
sitting in a chair...

706
00:43:22,734 --> 00:43:24,745
she fainted.

707
00:43:24,769 --> 00:43:28,381
You stepped out of the shadows,

708
00:43:28,405 --> 00:43:31,518
wiped your fingerprints
off the letter opener

709
00:43:31,542 --> 00:43:33,554
you had used to kill Marsha,

710
00:43:33,578 --> 00:43:36,590
and then placed
it in Allison's hand.

711
00:43:36,614 --> 00:43:39,259
Then you removed the necklace

712
00:43:39,283 --> 00:43:42,585
and cigarette holder
from Marsha's body.

713
00:43:48,460 --> 00:43:49,837
Doesn't prove anything, does it?

714
00:43:49,861 --> 00:43:51,741
No.

715
00:43:53,331 --> 00:43:54,864
The necklace.

716
00:43:56,300 --> 00:44:00,414
Now, we know the
real necklace is gone.

717
00:44:00,438 --> 00:44:01,771
This one...

718
00:44:02,841 --> 00:44:05,085
is a fake.

719
00:44:05,109 --> 00:44:07,943
But it was kept in the
safe in Allison's room.

720
00:44:11,816 --> 00:44:13,060
The cigarette holder is kept

721
00:44:13,084 --> 00:44:17,531
in Allison's jewelry
box, in her drawer.

722
00:44:17,555 --> 00:44:22,269
After the event with
Allison and Marsha...

723
00:44:22,293 --> 00:44:27,374
you put the necklace
back in the safe.

724
00:44:27,398 --> 00:44:29,977
And mistake.

725
00:44:30,001 --> 00:44:34,148
Tsk. You put this
in there with it.

726
00:44:34,172 --> 00:44:35,737
I saw it there.

727
00:44:40,111 --> 00:44:41,588
That doesn't prove
anything, does it?

728
00:44:41,612 --> 00:44:42,945
No.

729
00:44:45,416 --> 00:44:46,660
How about this?

730
00:44:46,684 --> 00:44:49,796
This is the butt...

731
00:44:52,557 --> 00:44:55,403
of one of those rare,
expensive cigars

732
00:44:55,427 --> 00:44:57,104
that you smoke.

733
00:44:57,128 --> 00:44:59,106
I bet you don't remember...

734
00:44:59,130 --> 00:45:02,576
throwing it in a
fireplace a few feet

735
00:45:02,600 --> 00:45:04,833
from Marsha Gold's body.

736
00:45:07,038 --> 00:45:08,516
But that's where it was found.

737
00:45:08,540 --> 00:45:11,218
By the police. And it's yours.

738
00:45:11,242 --> 00:45:13,020
The tobacco is the same...

739
00:45:13,044 --> 00:45:15,823
as one of your fresh ones.

740
00:45:15,847 --> 00:45:19,793
And no one besides
Allison, Marsha Gold,

741
00:45:19,817 --> 00:45:21,328
and whoever smoked this cigar,

742
00:45:21,352 --> 00:45:23,112
went in the library afterwards.

743
00:45:25,189 --> 00:45:28,001
Allison did see your
reflection in the mirror...

744
00:45:28,025 --> 00:45:30,659
in the library that night...

745
00:45:32,764 --> 00:45:34,497
when the lightning flashed.

746
00:45:36,467 --> 00:45:37,733
And this proves it.

747
00:45:42,840 --> 00:45:44,873
Don't you sometimes
wish you didn't smoke?

748
00:45:50,782 --> 00:45:52,425
Ladies and
gentlemen of the jury,

749
00:45:52,449 --> 00:45:53,827
have you reached a verdict?

750
00:45:53,851 --> 00:45:55,463
Yes, we have, Your Honor.

751
00:45:55,487 --> 00:45:58,298
We find the defendant,
Allison Tate, not guilty.

752
00:45:59,924 --> 00:46:02,335
Oh, thank you so much.

753
00:46:02,359 --> 00:46:04,505
Oh, God.

754
00:46:04,529 --> 00:46:05,862
Thank you so much.

755
00:46:09,600 --> 00:46:11,945
Warren? Warren.

756
00:46:11,969 --> 00:46:14,314
Warren.

757
00:46:14,338 --> 00:46:16,449
Well, weren't you
gonna say goodbye?

758
00:46:16,473 --> 00:46:20,687
Ah. I hate saying
goodbye. All right?

759
00:46:20,711 --> 00:46:22,211
I do it so badly. I...

760
00:46:23,481 --> 00:46:24,914
Then will you call?

761
00:46:26,017 --> 00:46:26,994
Yeah.

762
00:46:27,018 --> 00:46:28,162
Okay. I'll do that.

763
00:46:28,186 --> 00:46:29,485
Thanks for everything.

764
00:46:32,956 --> 00:46:34,396
Bye-bye. Take it easy.

765
00:46:38,796 --> 00:46:39,829
How about a hot dog?

766
00:46:40,965 --> 00:46:43,376
I accept. For old times' sake.

767
00:46:43,400 --> 00:46:44,400
All right.

768
00:46:45,603 --> 00:46:48,715
We, uh... We didn't
do too badly together.

769
00:46:48,739 --> 00:46:50,951
You ever think of
relocating up north?

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
